Author the **Caldera basalt** material — a tileable, seamless PBR material of
weathered volcanic rock — using only the material tools.

It dresses the terraced hex terrain of *Caldera*, a volcanic tower-defense case:
the cooled-lava walls and floors of a caldera, applied to the meshed terrain by
**triplanar projection**. You are authoring a *repeating surface*, not an object —
a set of maps that tiles seamlessly across the terrain, not a single rock.

## How authoring works

The only way to make a map is the two binaries already on your `PATH`. `texture`
is the seamless raster painter — it edits **one map at a time**, and every brush,
gradient, generator, and filter **wraps across the map edges**, so whatever you
paint tiles without a seam by construction (there is no separate "make seamless"
pass). `pbr` is the companion tool: it **bakes** the normal and ambient-occlusion
maps from a painted grayscale **height** field, **sets uniform** scalar maps,
**assembles** `material.json`, and **renders** a lit 3D preview of the assembled
material on a test surface. Both are the sole channel: a map produced any other
way (writing a PNG directly, editing a preview by hand) is discarded and flagged.

Every `texture` and `pbr` operation carries `--map <channel>` (defaulting to
`base-color`), and each channel is its own layered document at the case's size.
After each `texture` op the binary re-renders the active map's flat preview to
`maps/{map}.png`, shown **2x2-tiled** so seams show; `pbr render` writes the
one-on-request lit 3D preview. A blank set of maps and an empty log are already in
place at `{{workspace}}`, alongside a seeded `material.config.json` giving the map
size, the declared channels and tiling, and the store paths — so no operation
needs size flags.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, for example:

```
texture noise --map base-color --type fbm --scale 6 --octaves 4
pbr bake-normal --from height --strength 1.4
```

Run `texture --help` and `pbr --help` to list every operation, and `texture
<op> --help` / `pbr <op> --help` for one operation's exact flags — that help
text is the authoritative contract. **Read the previews after your calls** to see
your progress and decide what to do next.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build the material up operation by operation until it reads as the brief describes
— near-black weathered basalt cut by hairline ashen and faintly glowing fissures,
tiling seamlessly, with coherent relief, roughness, and occlusion. Author **only**
through the `texture` and `pbr` tools; paint every map so it wraps seamlessly; and
emit **exactly** the declared maps — `base-color`, `normal`, `roughness`, and
`ao`. When you are satisfied, stop: the emitted maps and their recorded
`actions.json` are your finished submission.
